KEYWORDS: environmental gas sensors, gas separation technologies, volatile organic compounds, photoionization detectors, electrochemical gas sensors, optical infrared sensors
O rganic substances known as volatile organic compounds (VOCs) have high vapor pressure at normal room temperature and pressure. Therefore, they make up a significant class of atmospheric pollutants, of which many are often categorized as hazardous air pollutants or toxins that are concerning to human health.1 The formation of exhaust gases, the evaporation of petroleum products, and the use of organic solvents all contribute to the formation of VOCs, which account for a significant portion of the gaseous pollutants in urbanized regions.2 Hydrocarbons, including aromatic hydrocarbons such as benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes (BTEX), as well as halogenated hydrocarbons such as dichloroethylene (DCE), trichloroethylene (TCE), and tetrachloroethylene (PCE), make up the major group of released substances. These VOCs have been determined to be carcinogenic and can impact human health and the entire ecosystem in the short and long term.3
VOCs tend to pollute water as well, making it unfit for consumption. Groundwater flowing through a contaminated area can carry VOC contaminants off-site, where VOC vapors can migrate vertically and laterally through the soil in a residential area and subsequently through building foundations to contaminate residential indoor air, a process known as vapor intrusion (VI).4 For VI site investigations, it is necessary to collect samples in designated containers or sorbent tubes and transfer them to a lab for gas chromatograph mass spectrometer (GC-MS) analysis when using current standard laboratory analytical procedures for the measurement of VOCs, such as the United States Environmental Protection Agency (US EPA) Methods TO-14 and TO-15. Because of its great sensitivity, precision, reproducibility, and general resilience, GC-MS is sometimes referred to as the gold standard for VOC analysis as it is especially able to analyze individual VOC species. Laboratory-based GC-MS techniques call for ex situ analysis and on-site indoor air sampling, both of which are unable to provide real-time VOC exposure data. Furthermore, when samples are stored or transferred to an analytical laboratory, targeted molecules may be lost due to vaporization or degradation.5 Rapid chemical analysis of VOCs on-site would reduce the chance of losing target analytes and be useful for quick assessments of health hazards. In-field analysis significantly reduces labor and commercial laboratory costs, while also speeding up the time it takes to obtain results. Recently, several studies have documented the use of portable GC-MS for characterizing and delineating contaminated sites.6,7 Although they are accurate and precise, the expense of acquisition, the difficulty of operation, and the time required for the analysis all prohibit the wider use of these systems, especially for network monitoring.
VI and the VOC results obtained from the field can differ by a huge margin due to spatial and temporal variations. These variations are due to various factors, such as attenuation of soil as it migrates upward, ambient and soil temperature, and changes in pressure and soil moisture content, etc., which impact field measurements.8 For example, fluctuations in subsurface soil temperature and moisture throughout the year have a significant influence on the assessment of VI. Recharge from rainfall events can trap contaminant vapors, thereby interfering with vapor sampling and measurement results. Soil moisture is a major factor in determining subsurface vapor attenuation. The VOC vapors are initially absorbed into the soil moisture and then become available for the soil microbial population, which then oxidizes the vapors mainly into carbon dioxide and water. Since the diffusivity of contaminants in the water-filled porosity is much less than that in the air-filled porosity, the soil moisture acts as a large resistance to diffusion. As the soil moisture increases, the effective air diffusivity decreases, resulting in additional partitioning to the liquid phase, thereby decreasing the gas concentration in the soil. The impact of air pressure and temperature on the VI process is noticeable and pronounced in shallow soil. A soil temperature rise of $\bar{5}~^{\circ}C$ was found to exacerbate the VI process, resulting in a 2-fold increase in indoor air tetrachloroethylene (PCE) concentration.8 An increased VI was observed during the summer when the higher soil temperature facilitates contaminant phase change from the aqueous phase to the gas phase, which drives the diffusion process.
As a result, there is a strong demand for gas detection equipment that is affordable, small, and simple to use for monitoring and evaluating long-term fluctuations/variations in VOC flux in soil vapor and groundwater in the field. Aligned with soil temperature, moisture and air pressure sensors, these systems can provide critical information and aid in understanding the relationship between the influencing factors and VOC vertical attenuation in a soil profile. A variety of sensing technologies have been applied for gas monitoring, including hazardous gas leak detection and ambient air monitoring. Photoionization detectors (PIDs) and optical infrared (IR) and electrochemical gas sensors, such as amperometric electrochemical sensors (AESs) and metal oxide semiconductors (MOSs), are commonly used technologies. There are many reviews on these sensors that focus on either current progress or the laboratory evaluation of their performance. Table 1 summarizes some of these reviews from the last five years. These sensors, whether nonselective or semiselective, can be influenced by unwanted analytes. To minimize interference, additional gas separation and discrimination techniques are frequently needed for sensors depending on their intended applications.
On the other hand, VOCs in air pollution can be heavily mixed in many situations. In the past decade, gas sensors have been utilized extensively for environmental monitoring to detect harmful VOCs present in emissions from various sources such as vehicles, factories, and power plants. In this regard, nitrogen oxides $\left(\mathrm{NOx}\right)$ , sulfur dioxide $\left(\operatorname{SO}_{2}\right)$ , ozone $(\bar{\textmd O}_{3})$ , and carbon monoxide (CO) are common VOCs that researchers often target. These compounds contribute significantly to air pollution, posing adverse effects on both human health and the environment. Numerous studies have been conducted to address these concerns and to develop more efficient gas-sensing technologies for the accurate and timely detection of these harmful VOCs. However, the use of gas sensors for monitoring hydrocarbon VOCs has not been extensively explored. For instance, in many TCE-contaminated sites, TCE is frequently combined with tetrachloroethylene (PCE) and its degradation products dichloroethene (DCE) and vinyl chloride (VC). For petroleum product contamination, soil gas may contain dozens of different aromatic VOCs, including BTEXs, trimethylbenzenes, butylbenzenes, and short-chain alkanes $C_{6}$ to ${\dot{\mathbf{C}}}_{16}$ .10 Table 2 shows the analytical results from dozens of VOCs detected in soil vapor samples (SVSs) collected from one of the diesel-contaminated sites. Monitoring the fluctuation trend of total VOCs for petroleum hydrocarbon-contaminated sites may not be able to show the trends of individual VOCs in these highly mixed samples. For example, the $C_{6}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{9}$ fraction can be attenuated more rapidly than the $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{10}}$ to $\mathbf{C}_{14}$ fraction, as the shorter the carbon chain is, the easier it is to break down. Therefore, the composition of the VOC mixture can vary significantly. To determine the individual VOCs in a highly mixed gas environment using nonselective or semiselective gas sensors, gas separation and discrimination technologies are needed. This focused review article discusses the most prominent gas separation and discrimination technologies for various types of gas sensors and their prospects in environmental applications. Subsequently, we express the authors’ perspectives regarding potential applications in the future.
## REVIEW
Various gas separation and discrimination technologies have been utilized to enhance the selectivity of nonselective or semiselective gas sensors and eliminate interference from unwanted gaseous components. Gas chromatography (GC) columns, gas permeable membranes, and bandpass filters are among the separation technologies that have been employed with PIDs, MOSs, AESs and IR sensors, respectively. The use of gas separation and discrimination technologies has been invaluable in environmental monitoring, site assessment, and workplace exposure assessments, where accurate and precise detection of targeted gases is crucial for ensuring human health and safety.
This review elaborates on various technologies primarily used in relevant sensors but is not limited to them. It is noteworthy that several gas technologies have been utilized across different types of sensors. For instance, gas separation membranes and metal−organic frameworks have been applied to all of the aforementioned sensors in this review.
## AMPEROMETRIC ELECTROCHEMICAL SENSORS
Amperometric electrochemical sensors (AESs) typically have 2 to 4 electrodes connected through an electrolyte medium. Target VOCs trigger redox reactions on the working electrode, causing a transduced electrical current to flow between the working and counter electrodes.2,9 The working and counter electrodes are usually polished disks or microelectrodes made of noble metals such as platinum, gold, or glassy carbon. A reference electrode, such as an $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ electrode, maintains a steady potential at the working electrode. Researchers have also explored other alternatives, such as polymers, ceramics, and nanocarbon materials, as working electrodes for their better sensitivity due to higher surface-area-to-volume ratios.11,12 Many reviews have addressed the current state of development of AESs.2,9,13−17 However, high synthesis costs must be addressed for commercial viability. AESs are preferred for their ease of use, reliability, and ability to operate at room temperature, making them safer and more energy-efficient.14,18
However, AESs face challenges such as cross-sensitivity and interference from other gases. The current gas separation technologies for AESs include gas separation membranes, metal−organic frameworks and microfluidics. Several reviews have discussed the recent advancements in these technologies, and some of them are highlighted in Table 3.
Gas Separation Membranes. The AES electrolyte enables ion movement between electrodes and contains ions for electrochemical reactions. It can be aqueous or nonaqueous and is essential for transporting electrical charges between the working and counter electrodes.19 Diluted sulfuric acid or organic solvents, such as acetonitrile or propylene carbonate, are commonly used as electrolytes in AESs.20 The performance and lifespan of these sensors are heavily influenced by temperature and humidity, which can affect the properties of the electrolytes. Gas permeable membranes, like Teflon films and silicon membranes, are commonly used to coat AESs to minimize the impact of environmental factors on the electrolyte, while still allowing gas analytes to pass through.21 Gas membranes can also serve as a means to improve the selectivity of AES by allowing only specific gases of interest to pass through based on different gas diffusion mechanisms.22
This makes it possible to selectively detect certain VOCs while excluding others.
Porous Membranes. Porous membranes, which are composed of polymers, metals, or oxides, act as molecular sieves and selectively allow smaller VOC molecules to permeate them.22−25 The surface chemistry of the membrane, including the thickness and porosity, can be optimized using machine learning methods to selectively target specific molecules of interest.26 Table 3 provides a summary of various reviews that cover the recent advancements in the fabrication of gas separation membranes. Toward this end, Sidhikku Kandath Valappil et al. conducted a comprehensive review in 2021 on commonly used polymer gas separation membranes, which include polyimides, poly(ethylene oxide), poly(ethylene glycol), and polydimethylsiloxane.25 At the same time, Agboola et al. reviewed polymer nanocomposite techniques in gas separation membrane (GSM) applications.24 Figure 1 demonstrates the schematic structure of gas separation membranes fabricated with nanomaterials.27 The fabrication methods using electrospraying and poly(lactic acid) were reviewed by Vatanpour et al. in 2022.28,29 Pal et al. summarized hydrogen separation membranes with various fabrication methods and different membrane supports.30 According to Kamble et al.’s review, mixed membranes show promise in improving the efficiency and selectivity of gas separation, as an alternative to using a single material for GSM fabrication.31
Porous membranes can selectively allow small VOC molecules to pass through. They can be fabricated using various materials such as polymers, metals, or oxides, as well as techniques such as electrospraying and poly(lactic acid). Despite their advantages, porous membranes may also permit the passage of unwanted molecules. Fouling is a limitation of these membranes that can decrease their effectiveness, necessitating frequent maintenance or replacement.
Dense Membranes. Porous membranes selectively allow smaller VOC molecules to permeate them, while dense or nanoporous membranes selectively filter gases based on both their size and chemical properties, relying on gas molecules to dissolve or diffuse through the material without having any pores or channels for gas flow.22,32 The latter can be tailored for specific applications, allowing only gases with certain properties to pass through while acting as barriers to others. This is useful for gas detection applications where discrimination between a broad range of gases is necessary. For example, it has been reported that only oxygen can flow through an acrylonitrile-butadiene copolymer membrane.21
The topic of dense or nanoporous membranes, made of polymers, ceramics or metals, has been extensively reviewed recently.25,30,33,34 Polymeric membranes are made of polymers like polyimides, polycarbonate and polyvinyl difluoride, which were used to separate gases like $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO},\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .25 Ceramic membranes made of crystal lattices can be applied in high temperatures or aggressive chemical environments, such as power plants and steel and glass industries,34 while metal membranes made of nanopalladium, niobium and tantalum are used for hydrogen purification and natural gas processing.30
Despite the advantages, dense or nanoporous membranes can be costly and complex to fabricate, and they are susceptible to fouling, which diminishes their effectiveness and necessitates frequent maintenance or replacement. Furthermore, while dense membranes can be tailored to specific applications, their selectivity is limited compared to porous membranes.
3D Gas Separation Network. Metal−Organic Frameworks. Recently, advanced porous materials, particularly coordination polymers or metal−organic frameworks (MOFs), have been widely studied as novel electrocatalysts for VOC separation.16,18 Crystalline and porous materials known as MOFs are composed of inorganic metal ions and organic ligands, which form a 3D network with nanometersized pores. A schematic illustration of MOFs, as demonstrated in Figure 2 from a manuscript by Newton Augustus et al., illustrates this arrangement.35 They selectively adsorb gas molecules based on their size, shape, and chemical properties, making them ideal for gas-selective sensing.18 The selective adsorption properties of MOFs have attracted many recent studies. For instance, Zhu and co-workers reported that a hydrophobic MOF sensor, based on MIL $(\mathrm{Cr}){-}Z1$ , has high adsorption capacity and selectivity for benzene.36 Similarly,
Cacho-Bailo et al. reported a method of separating the gases in which a MOF membrane inside a hollow polymeric fiber was used at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 4-bar pressure to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .37 Other recent achievements of MOFs in VOC adoption include cobalt-adenine,38 cobalt-macroporous carbon,39 copper-amino acid,40 etc. Kumar and co-workers have reviewed the application of MOFs in different types of electrochemical sensors.16 In addition, Shi et al. reviewed recent advancements and the future potential of MOF/polymer hybrid membranes for energy-efficient gas separations.41 As reviewed by Tang et al., MOFs have been utilized in gas sensing applications, such as in GC stationary phases for detecting hydrocarbon VOCs.42 Due to their high surface area and tunable pore size, MOFs hold promise in enhancing the separation efficiency of GC columns.
MOFs have many advantages, including a larger surface area, being recyclable, and able to be customized to specific various targeted gas molecules. Nevertheless, the thermal instability of MOFs can limit their use in catalysis and other applications. Additionally, MOF synthesis is a complex and time-consuming process that requires careful control of reaction conditions, resulting in costly production. Ongoing research aims to develop more cost-effective synthesis methods, which would help to mitigate this limitation.
Covalent Organic Frameworks. Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are organic porous materials with a 3D network structure composed of organic building blocks linked by covalent bonds. COFs have the potential to be used as materials for gas separation due to their high surface area and adjustable pore sizes, which have been extensively reviewed.43−45
Fabricating COF membranes with precise pore size, shape, and distribution is critical for gas separation, but the complex and delicate synthesis process makes it challenging. Uniform and defect-free COF layers on support surfaces are necessary for high selectivity and permeability. Considering the material properties of COFs, Wang et al. reviewed the conventional reactions employed for the synthesis of COFs.44 Similarly, Caro and colleagues fabricated $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ -supported COF-LZU1- ACOF-1 bilayer membranes using an in situ growth method, as shown in Figure 3.43 The method involved treating the $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ disk with 3-aminopropyl)triethoxysilane (APTES), poly[(9,9- dioctylfluorenyl-2,7-diyl)-co-( $^{\cdot}4,4^{\prime}$ -( $N\mathrm{.}$ -(4-sec-butylphenyl)- diphenylamine)] (TFB), and a mixture of $p$ -phenylenediamine (PDA) and hydrazine hydrate. The resultant bilayer membrane exhibited high selectivity and permeability for gas separation due to the interlaced pores close to the size of gas molecules and the thin COF-COF layer of approximately $1\mu\mathrm m$ thickness.
COFs share similarities with MOFs, such as having a large surface area and tunable porosity. However, COFs have higher thermal stability, making them applicable in fields where MOFs are not suitable, such as catalysis. Despite these advantages, the synthesis of COFs can also be challenging and timeconsuming, and they may have low mechanical stability.
Microfluidics. Microfluidics is an emerging technology that is currently well established in creating a whole laboratory setup on a single chip, known as a “lab-on-a-chip”. Microfluidic devices are gaining more interest in the field of environmental monitoring because of their smaller size, point of source detection and ease of fabrication. The separation of various gases using microfluidic devices is also gaining attention, as bulky instrumentation has issues pertaining to bulky hardware. Many researchers have reviewed the application in gas separation (Table 3). Various coating materials such as MOFs, polymers, zeolites, and microchannel designs have been explored for applications.43,46 According to Balakrishnan et al., these materials can be integrated into microfluidic devices using 3D printing technologies.47 3D printing allows for more flexibility in design and can significantly reduce the time and cost of fabrication. In addition, microfluidic devices can also be paper-based with membrane integration. For instance, Phansi et al.48 used a paper-based microfluidic analytical device for the detection of specific ammonia in a fish pond. In another interesting work, Sitanurak et al. reported a membrane-less gas separation microfluidic paper-based analytical device for detecting hypochlorite in bleach.49 Likewise, Yong et al. reported gas separation from a liquid using a microfluidic device in which the mesh was fabricated using a femtosecond laser.50 The absence of a membrane in these devices also simplifies the separation process and eliminates the need for maintenance. Overall, it can be concluded that microfluidicsintegrated gas separation can also be a plausible and effective solution for obtaining high selectivity in gas sensing.
Microfluidic gas separation offers several advantages, including high separation efficiency due to precise gas flow control, compact portability, reduced sample and reagent consumption, scalability, and multifunctionality. However, there are also limitations, including low gas flow rates, limited selectivity, limited durability due to potential clogging and failure, fabrication complexity, and higher cost compared to traditional methods.
## PHOTOIONIZATION DETECTORS
VOC molecules can be detected when they are ionized by high-energy photons, often in the UV range.9 PID sensors provide a nonspecific weighted response for all detectable chemical species, making them useful for detecting VOCs in a wide range of applications, including leak detection and environmental monitoring.51 Commercial PID sensors, such as the MiniPiD, piD-TECH, and UltraRAE 3000, are widely used for VOC detection. In 2021, Xu et al. compared the performance of 44 market-available PID sensors and found that they had high precision and rapid response times compared to MOS and EC sensors.1 In recent years, there has been a growing trend in employing chip-based microfluidics in the development of PIDs.52,53 In 2019, Rezende reported that many of the latest PID devices are designed to be compatible with microfluidic arrangements.51 By utilizing microfluidic channels to miniaturize the PID device, significant advantages such as faster response time, enhanced sensitivity, and reduced sample volume can be achieved.52
Ultraviolet Lamps. In UV lamp ionization, a gas sample is exposed to high-energy ultraviolet light, which causes the gas molecules to become ionized. This ionization generates a flow of electrons, which is detected by the PID sensor as an electrical current. The strength of this electrical current is proportional to the concentration of VOCs in the gas sample. Modern PID sensors typically use radio frequencies (RFs) to excite UV lamps for photoionization. These RF-excited lamps are smaller and more energy-efficient than previous technologies.54
Depending on the low-pressure gas fill, UV lamps operated by RFs produce photon energies ranging from $105\ \mathrm{nm}$ (11.8 eV) to $150\ \mathrm{nm}$ (8.4 eV).54 Xenon (Xe, 8.4 and $9.6\ \mathrm{\eV}$ ), deuterium (D $,10.2\:\mathrm{eV})$ , krypton $\left(\mathrm{Kr},10.6~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , and argon (Ar, $11.8~\mathrm{eV})$ are the most commonly encountered gas fillers. UV lamps with higher photon energy are less specific to VOC groups. Xenon lamps, for example, can only detect VOCs that have more than six carbon atoms. Argon lamps can detect nearly $80\%$ of VOCs. To detect more VOCs, other fillers, including water $(12.6~\mathrm{\eV})$ , acetonitrile $\left(12.2~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , liquid nitrogen $\left(14.5\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , and liquid helium $(23\ \mathrm{\eV})$ , have been reported.54 For example, Pang et al. recently developed a method of employing helium to produce a plasma that emits photos in the 13.5−17.5 eV range, which can identify the majority of VOCs.55
Common silicon-based glasses do not transmit UV light. Hence, a crucial component of UV lamps is the UV transmission window. Magnesium fluoride $(\mathrm{MgF}_{2})$ and lithium fluoride (LiF) crystals are widely used materials for these applications. $\mathrm{MgF}_{2}$ transmits UV light down to $110\mathrm{nm}$ or 11.3 $\mathrm{eV}$ and is the preferred material with high resistance to chemical etching, laser damage, and mechanical and thermal shock. Nevertheless, LiF crystals are required with UV lamps with higher energy (below $110\ \mathrm{nm}$ or higher than $11.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), such as argon. Because a sufficiently high transmission coefficient for light below $110\ \mathrm{nm}$ is necessary, high-energy UV lamps are based on argon and require LiF crystals. However, LiF is a hygroscopic substance that can dissolve in high humidity, and because of its thermal expansion property, achieving a good seal to prevent contamination of the lamp’s gas is challenging.
Microelectromechanical GC Columns. A recent development is the use of a miniaturized $(\mu)\mathrm{GC-PID}$ with microelectromechanical (MEM) systems, which offers several advantages over traditional systems, including reduced analysis time, decreased sample size requirements, and improved portability.56,57 In Figure 4 a recent advancement of $\mu\mathrm{GC}$ is depicted that utilizes three MEM devices reported by Zampolli et al.57 Several studies have utilized $\mu{\mathrm{GC-PID}}$ to examine the presence of VOCs, such as BTEX compounds, in diverse environmental settings worldwide, providing valuable information for environmental assessments and public health protection. For example, Frausto-Vicencio et al. conducted a performance evaluation of a $\mu\mathrm{GC}$ -PID system for near-realtime BTEX analysis and field deployment and determined that the system has the potential for on-site VOC monitoring.58 A $\mu\mathrm{GC}$ -PID capable of separating typical petroleum hydrocarbon VOCs with a detection limit of 0.14 ppt was developed by Wei-Hao Li et al. (see Figure 4.a and 4.b).59 Additionally, computational algorithms including machine learning have been utilized to enhance the detection of volatile organic compounds through $\mu{\mathrm{GC-PID}}.$ 0 These systems are particularly useful for on-site analysis in remote or difficult-to-access locations.
MEM GC columns offer fast and efficient gas separation in a portable and compact format, but their limitations such as sample capacity, selectivity, durability, and fabrication complexity should be taken into account before use.
## METAL OXIDE SEMICONDUCTORS
Metal oxide semiconductor (MOS) sensors are categorized as conductometric or resistive-type devices.3 These sensors work by heating metal oxide (MO) materials to a suitable temperature, causing the chemisorption of oxygen molecules to increase (p-type) or decrease (n-type) the electrical resistance.61 The response and sensitivity of transition MObased materials are determined by the adsorption−desorption interaction between the MO surface and VOCs. Common MO materials studied include $\mathrm{WO}_{3},$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3},$ CuO, and $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ . Moseley summarized many MO material applications, including both n-type and p-type applications, in different gas detections before 2017.62 Advanced materials such as graphene and reduced graphene oxide have also been applied for gas detection, which has been summarized by Toda et al.63 Despite their widespread use, MOS sensors are not without limitations, including their susceptibility to changes in humidity, temperature, and interference from other gases. To address these issues, gas separation technologies have been applied to MOSs. For instance, Song et al. developed a versatile gas-sensing system for detecting NO with exceptional precision at room temperature by fabricating CuMOF in TiO2 nanochannels.64
Surface Modifications. Surface modifications of MO materials have been explored as a means to improve their selectivity toward specific VOCs. These modifications include the incorporation of nanoparticles, nanofibers, or nanorod morphology structures.65 Moreover, the addition of noble metals such as Pt, Pd, Au, and Rh can enhance the sensitivity of MOS sensors. Rh-doped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanofibers, for example, have shown a 9.6 times higher response to acetone and ethanol than undoped $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanofibers.66 Recently, the modification of MO materials to improve their sensitivity toward targeted petroleum hydrocarbon (PH) VOCs has emerged as another advance. For example, the toluene selectivity of $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ sensors can be significantly increased by introducing PdO or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},^{67}$ while acetylene selectivity can be improved by adding Pt or $\mathrm{Ag.}^{68}$ CuO nanoparticle-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanochannels were reported by Song et al. as a remarkably sensitive sensor for detecting $\mathrm{\dot{H}}_{2}S$ .69 In the same way, $\mathtt{X u}$ et al. applied Au modified $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanofiber sensor for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ detection.70 Gao et al. reported a $\mathrm{WO}_{3}$ nanoplate MOS sensor with significantly higher selectivity for cyclohexene than for other VOCs, such as benzene, ethanol, and acetone.71
It is worth noting that polyoxometalates (POMs) and colloidal quantum dots (CQDs) have been investigated as sensing materials to improve the selectivity and sensitivity of MOSs. POMs are a class of inorganic compounds composed of metal cations and oxygen anions that have tunable electronic and catalytic properties, while CQDs are nanocrystals of semiconductors with unique optical and electrical properties, including high quantum yield, tunable bandgap, and high surface-area-to-volume ratio. Both POMs and CQDs can be designed to detect a targeted gas, including VOCs, nitrogen oxides, and carbon monoxide, with high sensitivity and selectivity. Their applications have been reviewed by Tang et al.73 and Song et al.,72 respectively.
Operational Temperature. Recent studies have shown that indistinguishable VOCs can be distinguished by using the same MOS sensor at different temperatures.3 The reason for this is that the interaction between VOCs and the MOS sensor requires the presence of specific oxygen species on the surface of the MO grains, which only occurs at distinct temperatures. For example, most MOS sensors respond to ethanol, acetone, benzene, and toluene indistinguishably.74 However, it was reported that indistinguishable VOCs can be distinguished by using the same MOS sensor at different temperatures.3 Wang et al. reported that their $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ sensor can detect benzene and $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ at 230 and $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively.75 Xing and co-workers reported that a silver-decorated indium oxide sensor had a distinguishable reaction for acetone and ethanol at 250 and $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively.76 The operational temperature necessitates careful calibration for better accuracy. Meanwhile, MOSs may be vulnerable to contamination in harsh environments, which has an adverse effect on their reliability and precision.
eNoses. In recent years, there has been a growing trend toward the application of MOS-type sensor arrays knonwn as electronic noses (eNoses) for discriminating gas mixtures.77 To tackle the issue of cross-sensitivity, scientists have combined MOS sensor arrays with artificial neural networks (ANNs) and other chemometric approaches. This allows for more accurate detection and identification of multiple analytes.77
For example, Conti et al. developed eNose systems composed of different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sensors and applied ANNs to distinguish between ethanol, acetone and formaldehyde.78 The system showed high accuracy and was able to differentiate between the target VOCs even in the presence of interfering compounds. In a study by Shooshtari et al., an eNose system consisting of eight different carbon nanotube- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ sensors was developed and applied to distinguish between four different VOCs, namely, acetone, butanol, ethanol and propanol, with an accuracy of 96%.79
Moreover, MOS-type sensor arrays coupled with chemometric methods have been widely employed for odor pattern recognition in different practical applications, as summarized by Hu and co-workers.80 Some recent applications include that of Khorramifar and co-workers, who applied an array of nine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ sensors for potato cultivar classification and identification based on odors.81 This research group also utilized the same eNose for plant oil discrimination,82 with ANN applied in both studies. Nonetheless, eNoses based on MOS-type sensor arrays for gas mixture discrimination may need frequent calibration to maintain accuracy as the sensors degrade over time, particularly when exposed to harsh environmental conditions.
## INFRARED SENSORS
In electrically noisy conditions where electronic sensors cannot function well, infrared sensors (IRSs) based on the interaction of light with gas can provide an alternative sensor type. Many chemical species exhibit strong absorption in the light regions from the near-IR $\left(700\ \mathrm{nm}{-}2.5\ \mu\mathrm{m}\right)$ to mid-IR $(2.5-25~\mu\mathrm{m})$ spectrum.83,84 According to ref 85, the high absorption capacity of hydrocarbons and other pollutants within the atmospheric transparency window ranging from 3 to $4~\mu\mathrm{m}$ renders this spectral region particularly suitable for environmental monitoring. The unique absorption peaks observed in the IR region are characteristic of the functional groups present in different chemical species. This property serves as the basis for the detection and measurement of these species using infrared sensors.86
In an IRS system, the transmitter and receiver are the two independent parts. The transmitter emits light, which is reflected by the targeted VOCs in a gas cell and then detected by the receiver.8 The transmitter can utilize various IR transmitting technologies, such as thermal emitters with MEMS,88,89 light-emitting diodes (LEDs),90 and quantum cascade lasers (QCLs).91 On the other hand, the IR receiver plays a critical role in detecting the reflected light and analyzing its spectra. Acoustic and optical detections are the two main categories of IR receivers that have been extensively reviewed.83,92 Acoustic detection relies on the absorption of sound waves by the targeted VOCs, while optical detection measures the changes in light intensity caused by the absorption of IR radiation. Furthermore, IRS miniaturization and lab-on-chip integration have also been investigated and reported.84 These new developments in IR transmitting technologies have shown promising results in terms of improved performance and reduced cost.
Bandpass Filters. IR thermal emitters fabricated from silicon wafers or other MEMS technologies emit broadband IR sources without dispersive elements such as diffraction gratings or prisms.93 For this type of nondispersive IR sensor, bandpass IR interference filters or waveguides with sufficiently narrow bandwidths can be applied to detect and segregate the targeted VOCs. As described previously, the distinct absorption peaks in the IR region are specific to each species, and a narrow-band filter can be used to remove undesired interference by only allowing the IR signal in the desired region to pass. There are two types of bandpass filters: interference filters and waveguide filters.
Interference Filters. An IR interference filter is a device that selectively transmits a specific wavelength range of IR radiation and reflects all other wavelengths. It is composed of alternating layers of different materials with different refractive indices.94 The common semiconducting materials applied include metalloids, such as arsenic (As), silicon (Si), germanium (Ge), antimony (Sb), and tellurium (Te); post-transition metals, such as aluminum (Al), gallium $\left(\mathbf{Ga}\right)$ , indium $\left(\mathrm{In}\right)$ , and tin $(\mathsf{S}\mathrm{n})$ ; and sometimes reactive nonmetals, such as selenium (Se), bromine (Br), and iodine(I). IR interference filters can be fabricated using various methods, such as vacuum deposition, sputtering, and ion-assisted deposition, which has been recently reviewed by Lochbaum et al.89 The thickness of each layer is carefully designed to create a specific interference pattern that selectively enhances the transmission of the desired wavelength range and reduces the transmission of other wavelengths.95 For example, Duan et al. developed a sixchannel integrated narrow-band mid-IR filter $\left(3.6{-}4.4~\mu\mathrm{m}\right)$ using different thicknesses of Ge film on Si, which is shown in Figure 5.96 Shi et al. developed a tunable narrow-band mid-IR bandpass filter of $3.1-4.8\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ based on surface lattice resonance using different thicknesses of Ge−Sb−Te.97
For near-IR filters, Mirnia and Lail reported a tunable nearIR bandpass filter based on a hybrid graphene−nanometallic structure.98 Jeong and Mang proposed another visible-to-nearIR narrow bandpass filter with cholesteric liquid crystal materials.99 Mid-IR filters are more difficult to produce than visible/near-IR filters due to their greater thickness, which also makes striping the photoresist during the lift-off process challengin g.100 Based on the Fabry−Perot interferometer, midIR filters have been developed by layering a fine-tuned thin film (nanometers) of semiconducting materials stacked on top of one another to form quantum well heterostructures or surface lattices. Sieger and Mizaikoff summarized the recent development of mid-IR transmitting materials for thin-film waveguide fabrication.84
IR interference filters offer a cost-effective solution for gas separation and analysis, and their integration into various IR devices is straightforward. In contrast, IR interference filters may have some drawbacks such as sensitivity to temperature changes and limited durability, which might necessitate frequent replacement or maintenance. Moreover, they may not offer an adequate resolution for highly intricate gas mixtures.
Hollow-Core Waveguide. Hollow-core waveguide optical fibers (HCWOFs) can be fabricated using a variety of materials, including metals, dielectrics, and chalcogenide glasses, depending on the desired wavelength range and application.101 Figure 6 demonstrates a revolver hollow-core optical fiber made of As30Se50Te20 glass, reported by Bufetov et al.102 In the mid-IR region, chalcogenide glass-based optical fibers can deliver wavelengths in the range of $4-12\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ .103,104 For example, Te−As−Se chalcogenide glass HCWOF has been used to provide an IR wavelength of $10.6\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ detection .103 Other materials used for HCWOFs include $_\mathrm{Ge-Se}$ and $\mathrm{Ga{-}G e{-}T e{-}I}$ glasses for wavelengths of $5\mathrm{-}7.5$ $\mu\mathrm{m}$ and 10.5−13.5 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively.105 Additionally, HCWOFs have been fabricated using polyethylene terephthalate glycol for light delivery in the range of ${\dot{3}}.5{-}5\ \mu\mathrm{m},^{\mathrm{\tiny{106}}}$ fluoride $\left(\mathrm{ZrF4}\right)$ glass fibers that deliver wavelengths up to 4 $\mu\mathrm{m},^{107}$ sfiolrv lide epcotliyocnr.y10st8alline fibers for a wavelength of $10.6~\mu\mathrm{m}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$
An antiresonant hollow-core microgap waveguide is a novel pdlisatfaonrcems fon  icnhvieps.t1i0g9,a1t1i0ngI lciognhts-isgtass finhtoelrlaoctwi-ocno osvegrmleontgs separated by gaps that allow external access to the core region, enabling the introduction of gases for sensing applications. The design of the waveguide incorporates antiresonance effects to achieve broadband transmission and high-contrast resonances for sensing. The waveguide is implemented on silicon chips using 3D-nano printing based on two-photon absorption.109 This allows for the creation of samples with lengths up to ${\mathfrak{s}}\mathrm{cm}$ and dimensions controlled with step sizes of $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The structure also has an exceptionally high structural openness factor of $18\%$ , which allows for efficient gas or liquid diffusion through the waveguide.110
Li et al. reviewed HCWOF-based gas sensors and found that they offer relatively high sensitivity and immunity to electromagnetic interference.101 However, their complex and costly fabrication process limits their implementation. Additionally, HCWOFs may be less tolerant of bending and more vulnerable to changes in temperature and pressure.
IR Transmitters. Quantum cascade lasers (QCLs) and IR light-emitting diodes (LEDs) are two types of infrared transmitters commonly used in various applications, including gas sensing and spectroscopy. QCLs and LEDs are both designed with multiple layers of semiconductor materials with different thicknesses and compositions, which allow them to emit at precise wavelengths to target specific gas analytes.111,112 Figure 7 demonstrates different IR transmitters with their corresponding wavelength ranges. IR LEDs are more costeffective to fabricate, but with lower resolution than QCLs.
Quantum Cascade Lasers. QCLs are semiconductor lasers that emit at specific wavelengths in the mid-IR region. They consist of multiple layers of semiconductor materials, with each layer having a different thickness and composition. Electrons flow through the device and are confined to specific energy levels within the material, known as quantum wells. As the electrons move between the different wells, they release energy in the form of photons, which create the laser beam.113 QCLs are designed to emit at specific wavelengths to target desired gas analytes using plasmonic surface lattice resonance. Even at room temperature, QCLs can generate watts of output power, cover the majority of the mid-IR spectrum between 3 and 25 $\mu\mathrm{m},$ and extend farther into the far-IR region between 1 and 5 $\mathrm{THz}$ .114 For example, a mid-IR QCL made of In-As/In-As-Sb provides wavelengths of 4.2 and $4.7\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ to detect carbon dioxide (CO) and carbon monoxide;85 an Al-In-As/In-As-Sbbased QCL emits $3.9{-}4.9~\mu\mathrm{m}$ IR to detect $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(4.2~\mu\mathrm{m})$ , $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ $(4.5~\mu\mathrm{m})$ and CO $(4.{\dot{6}}\ \mu\mathrm{m});^{94}$ and an Al-In-Sb/In-Sbbased QCL is used for methane detection.95 Other IR QCLs include Ge-Sn/Si-Ge-Sn-based to provide wavelengths of $2.1-$ 2.3, 2.9−3.2, and 4.35 μm.96,97
Light-Emitting Diodes. IR light-emitting diodes (LEDs), on the other hand, are semiconductor devices that emit light when an electrical current is applied. Different materials and structures can be used to target specific wavelengths within this range; for example, gallium antimonide (GaSb) was used for the emission of $2-3\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ and GaSb/aluminum gallium arsenide (AlGaAs) was used for the emission of around 3.3 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ .115,116 Di ́az-Thomas et al. presented both a schematic and a scanning electron microscope (SEM) image depicting a cross section of their infrared LED, which is shown in Figure 8.116 IR LEDs have advantages for gas separation, such as costeffectiveness, ease of fabrication, low power consumption, long lifetime, and miniaturization. They are suitable for portable gas sensors and can be integrated with other electronic components. However, their limited output power is a disadvantage, leading to lower sensitivity and detection limits compared to other IR sources such as quantum cascade lasers.
IR Receivers. Quartz Tuning Forks. Instead of using an optical detector, acoustic detectors, based on the IR photoacoustic effect, have also been widely studied. Stefan Palzer reviewed recent photoacoustic-based gas sensing applications.92 With the application of a quartz tuning fork (QTF) as a sound transducer, the photoacoustic signal must coincide with the right frequency to the resonance of the QTF system, which can be considered a bandpass filter.92 QTFs operate by detecting sound waves generated by the photoacoustic effect (Figure 9). For instance, Elefante et al. reported that their customized QTF could be excited at $7.73\ \mu\mathrm{{m}}$ (first overtone flexural mode) to target methane and nitrous oxide absorption peaks.117 Acetylene detection has also been reported at 1.53 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ with a QTF-based IR gas sensor,118 and carbon monoxide detection has been reported with a $15.2\ \mathrm{kHz}$ QTF at 4.6 $\mu\mathrm{m}$ .119 However, based on our literature review, the QTF technique is still applied as a transducer instead of a light filter. Either a QCL (mid-IR) or diode laser (near-IR) is employed to provide the specific IR regions.117−119 When compared to other IR wavelength filters, including QCLs and HCWOFs, QTFs may have lower resolutions, but they are more costeffective and simple to fabricate.
## PERSPECTIVE AND DISCUSSION
Table 4 summarizes the gas separation efficiency, fabrication complexity, durability, and maintenance of the different technologies mentioned above. Many technologies, such as MOF, microfluidics, and MEM GC columns offer high gas separation efficiency but high fabrication complexity and moderate durability. Surface modification of MOSs provides moderate efficiency and resolution with similar fabrication complexity and durability but differs in maintenance and calibration requirements. For PID detectors, UV lamps have low efficiency and resolution with low to moderate fabrication complexity and durability but require moderate maintenance and calibration. Infrared gas sensors, such as interference filters and HCWOFs, provide moderate to high efficiency and resolution with low to moderate fabrication complexity and durability and require low maintenance and calibration. Meanwhile, LEDs and QCLs offer low to moderate efficiency and resolution with low fabrication complexity, low to moderate durability, and low maintenance and calibration requirements. Finally, QTFs provide moderate to high efficiency and resolution with low to moderate fabrication complexity, high durability and stability, and low maintenance and calibration requirements.
GSM, MOF, and COF. Gas filters that separate molecules are useful in environmental applications. Chlorinated solvent sites, for instance, benefit from molecular sieves since they can partition TCE away from PCE, DCE, and VC, all of which often coexist at contaminated locations. Molecular sieves also serve to categorize petroleum VOC mixtures by their fractions. This process allows compounds like BTEX to be singled out as hazardous VOCs distinctively amidst a gas mixture identified as total VOC with greater accuracy than before. This is particularly important for ensuring workers’ safety and maintaining environmental standards.
To achieve even greater accuracy in filtering out undesired gas molecules, multiple layers of gas separation filters can be employed. This approach involves using different types of filters with varying levels of porosity to target specific gas molecules at different stages of the filtration process. For instance, a porous filter can be used to first remove larger gas molecules before a denser filter or MOF is applied to filter out smaller, more complex molecules.
In recent years, considerable attention has been given to the study of GSMs and MOFs. This focus has primarily centered on enhancing their performance capabilities while also investigating new applications for these materials. For example, research conducted by Zhang et al. indicates that gas sensing systems using COF-and-MOF-based technology are in the experimental phases and face significant challenges before commercial use can even be considered due to having weak electrical conductivity issues.120 To promote commercialization efforts, specifically those related to fabricating costeffective yet scalable gas separation filters, researchers should explore various novel fabrication methods like electrospinning or additive manufacturing techniques that are capable of producing large quantities of both COFs and MOs at a time. The recent fabrication methods have been reviewed by Vatanpour et al.28,29 Additionally, according to Wang et al., fabrication efficiency can be further improved by using optimization methods such as machine learning.26
$\mu G C-P I D$ and Advanced UV Lamps. UV Lamps. Gas separation using different UV lamps can be useful to determine hydrocarbons in petroleum gaseous mixtures. There are many organic compounds commonly present in petroleum products, including paraffin, naphthenes, and aromatics. Alkanes or paraffin are typically found in the highest concentration in most petroleum products. PH fractions are defined based on a range of physical−chemical properties and simple partitioning models, and each fraction contains many individual chemicals. It is a common practice to use carbon fractions to classify PHs in high mixtures. Equivalent carbon numbers $\operatorname{\rho}(\operatorname{Cn})$ , or the length of the carbon chain, are used to describe the aliphatic fractions.121 The highly volatile hydrocarbon fractions $({\mathsf{C}}6-$ C14) evaporate into the gas phase of the porous medium and may be lost to the atmosphere, adsorbed onto soil solids, or dissolved into soil water or groundwater. Based on the theory that xenon lamps can detect VOCs with more than six carbons (C6), the total recoverable hydrocarbons and C7−C16 fraction can be determined. This separation is important because most of the hazardous compounds that contain one or two benzene rings are in this PH fraction. The PH fractions C2−C6 and $_{\mathrm{C7-C16}}$ are expected to be determined by combining PID sensors with lower photon energy lamps, such as krypton and argon, which can present the total VOCs.
In the future, materials with UV transmission windows should be further studied for application in UV lamps with energies higher than $10.3\ \mathrm{\eV}$ . The replacement of lithium fluoride crystals with new materials needs to be addressed by researchers. It has been shown that film-coating materials can both improve the PID photon efficiency and prevent degradation.54 Hence, film coating of LiF crystals may also be an option to protect the crystal from humidity and improve its lifetime.
It is also worth considering the combination of PID with various UV lamps and customized gas filters to enhance gas discrimination. UV lamps work by filtering gas molecules based on photon energy, while gas membranes and organic frameworks function by separating gases based on their size and chemical properties. The combination of these techniques could lead to better gas separation and discrimination, particularly with the aid of machine learning for optimization purposes.
$\mu G C-P I D$ . PID-type sensors applied as either a handheld device or a monitoring station have been widely accepted by the environmental industry for rapid in situ VOC detection. According to the literature, the detection limit of PID-type sensors can reach approximately $1{-}10~\mathrm{ppb}$ , which is similar to the detection limit of conventional GC-FID or GC-MS.2 PIDtype sensors with a GC column and programmable temperature controller are candidates to replace portable GC/MS to determine individual VOCs in a highly mixed gas sample, such as a PH-contaminated site. Compared to GC-FID or GC-MS, GC-PID is more affordable and compactable.
Although microfluidic PIDs have demonstrated promising results for gas detection and analysis in the lab, in our opinion, they are still in the development stage and have not yet been generally accepted for commercialization. Fortunately, it is foreseeable that handheld or pocket $\mu\mathrm{GC}$ -PID devices, powered by rechargeable batteries, will be developed and widely applied for field-based VOC detection. Smartphone applications will be developed to wirelessly operate $\mu{\mathrm{GC-PIDs}}$ with temperature programming and display the measurement results. For subppb or ppt level detection, preconcentration technology, such as solid-state microextraction fiber, can be applied for sampling and preconcentration. The preconcentration technologies have been widely applied to $\mu\mathrm{{\bar{G}C\mathrm{-}M S}}$ .6,7
Advanced MOS and eNose. According to the literature ,62,65 MO sensor arrays and data analysis algorithms, such as eNose, can aid in detecting multiple gases simultaneously. For electrochemical sensors, eNoses targeting VOCs should be further investigated. The combination of different types of MOS sensors in an array needs to be optimized based on the targeting analytes and possible interference. To further solve the cross-interference issue, eNoses with a temperature control system can be considered. An operational temperature sequence, for example, can be programmed to gradually increase from 200 to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . With the further development of MOS sensors and eNoses, the determination of VOCs in unknown mixtures can be achieved.
However, it is difficult to be convinced that semiselective sensor arrays could be applied to analyze the detailed composition of highly mixed VOCs from an unknown contamination source. With the eNose trend of bundling more sensors together, it is foreseeable that miniaturized MOS eNoses with nano-MO materials will be developed and all of the selected MO materials will be integrated into microelectronics and MEMs as a lab-on-a-chip. Nevertheless, due to the sensitivity and detection limit of current MOS technology, eNoses for odor discrimination are still the major application for the industry in the foreseeable future. In addition, the development of low-cost techniques for synthesizing MO materials, including sol−gel methods,122 hydrothermal synthesis,123 chemical vapor deposition,124 and electrodeposition, 125 can be the focus of future study.
One of the potential applications of eNoses is to determine the aging status of environmental contaminants. The contaminants released into the environment go through a range of physical, chemical, and biological processes that almost instantly start to change these contaminants, known as the aging or weathering process.3,4 For example, when PCE and TCE are discharged into the environment, they will further break down into DCE and then VC, which can then degrade into other products. In many scenarios, environmental contaminant sources are known, such as oil spills or chemical leakages, and the initial PH compounds in the petroleum product can be predetermined using the conventional GC−MS method. By knowing the aging status, the remaining compounds can be predicted using models trained by the comprehensive data collected from GC−MS. For example, eNose was applied as a pattern recognition technique for the discrimination of weathered petroleum-derived products by Aliaño-González et al.126
Waveguided IR Detections. For IR gas sensors, the working principle is to use the IR frequency domain for organic functional group determination. This type of sensor application is greatly hampered by its low sensitivity and signalto-noise ratio. It is difficult to detect VOCs at lower than the ppm level. In our opinion, even with bandpass filters and other gas separation technologies, these sensors are unable to classify substances with the same functional groups. For example, IR sensors have difficulty distinguishing between DCE, TCE, and PCE, as they all contain carbon−carbon double bonds and carbon−chloride bonds. For total PH VOC detection, on the other hand, which has fewer sensitivity requirements, IR sensors can be helpful. By applying multiple band filters in the mid-IR range from 2.9 to $3.6\ \mu\mathrm{m},$ , IR sensors can be used to determine total PH VOCs. Furthermore, the section of $3.3-$ $3.6~\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ for $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds can be applied for saturated hydrocarbon compounds, such as alkanes; and the section of $2.9\mathrm{-}3.3~\mu\mathrm{m}$ for $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds can be applied for alkene, alkyne, and aromatic compound determination.
Miniaturized IRSs attached to or integrated into smart devices are expected to have many prospects; however, low cost and power consumption are still issues.83 From our understanding, both QCLs and IR LEDs are likely to see improvements in power output and efficiency, enabling them to be used in a wider range of environmental monitoring applications. As with many electronic devices, QCLs and IR LEDs are likely to become smaller and more compact in the future. QCLs and IR LEDs may be integrated with other technologies, such as MEMS or photonic integrated circuits (PICs), to create more powerful and versatile devices. Similar to bandpass filtering technologies, researchers are constantly working to expand the range of wavelengths that can be generated by QCLs and IR LEDs. According to Palzer, QTFs can also be further miniaturized and integrated into MEMs, PICs, or lab-on-a-chip systems.92 As mentioned by Sampaolo et al, QTFs have the potential to be used to detect multiple gases simultaneously.127 Future work may focus on developing QTF-based gas sensors that can detect a wide range of gases with high sensitivity and selectivity.
## CONCLUSION
Gas separation and discrimination technologies have been applied to increase the selectivity of current gas sensors by eliminating undesired gaseous components. However, these technologies are mainly evaluated in laboratory-controlled environments and have yet to be widely used in the field for vapor intrusion monitoring. Microfluidic devices are gaining interest in environmental monitoring due to their small size, point-of-source detection, and ease of fabrication, but challenges remain in terms of scalability and reliable microfabrication techniques. In the future, gas discrimination and separation technologies could be further developed to separate gaseous components in more complicated mixtures in the field. Compared to GC-FID or GC-MS, GC-PID is a more costeffective and portable option for VOC detection, and miniaturized GC-PID devices are expected to become widely used in the field. Analyzing the precise composition of highly mixed VOCs from an unidentified source of contamination with semiselective electrochemical sensors is challenging, but molecular sieves and gas permeable membranes can be used to reduce interference from large undesirable molecules. While IR sensors have low sensitivity and signal-to-noise ratio, they can be useful for total PH VOC detection, which has lower sensitivity requirements. However, it is difficult to detect VOCs at levels lower than ppm using IR sensors.
